@article{TOMASZ2025S230,
 abstract = {Objective
To present an interesting imaging technique that utilizes multiscan technology.
Introduction
In the examination of the musculoskeletal system, we often encounter extensive changes that are difficult to capture using traditional ultrasound examinations. An intriguing method for imaging such pathologies is the compilation of a series of images in a specialized program that integrates the various scanning layers.
Materials and Methods
By using an attachment that reads tissue layers and a program that integrates the locations of structures, we can construct a matrix comprising successive overlapping layers, allowing us to visualize extensive areas of interest to the researcher.
Results
In a series of tissue scans, we initially combined two scans, then three, and now four - provided they are not overly complex. In an advanced version of the program, we have merged seven layers. I speculate that with the advancement of technology, it may become possible to further combine scans and analyze larger areas of the human body with near - anatomical precision.
Conclusions
Multiscan may prove useful in everyday medical practice, especially in situations where many expensive imaging studies, such as computed tomography or magnetic resonance imaging, are not readily available. It has the potential to become a common and affordable diagnostic tool.},
 author = {Kardacz TOMASZ},
